<stack-static>
    <statement><p><p>For each statement, choose the method that its form points to. Use the questions in the box above: is it true, is it about a finite number of cases, is each case built from the one before, is it of the form ‘if A, then B’, or does it say that something is impossible?</p>
<ol>
<li><p>The sequence with <m>u_1 = 4</m> and <m>u_{n+1} = 3u_n - 2</m> satisfies <m>u_n = 3^n + 1</m> for every positive integer <m>n</m>.</p>
<div>[ direct proof | proof by exhaustion | proof by induction | proof by contradiction | disproof by counterexample ]  </div></li>
<li><p>For every positive integer <m>n</m>, <m>2^n + 1</m> is prime.</p>
<div>[ direct proof | proof by exhaustion | proof by induction | proof by contradiction | disproof by counterexample ]  </div></li>
<li><p>For every integer <m>n</m> with <m>1 \le n \le 6</m>, <m>n^2 - n + 11</m> is prime.</p>
<div>[ direct proof | proof by exhaustion | proof by induction | proof by contradiction | disproof by counterexample ]  </div></li>
<li><p>For every real number <m>x</m>, <m>x^2 - 6x + 10 \gt 0</m>.</p>
<div>[ direct proof | proof by exhaustion | proof by induction | proof by contradiction | disproof by counterexample ]  </div></li>
</ol></p></statement>
    <solution><p><p>Ask the questions in order. A false statement cannot be proved, so test first. A finite list of cases can be checked one by one. A statement about every positive integer whose cases build on each other suggests induction. ‘If A, then B’ suggests a direct proof (or a contradiction). A statement that something is impossible suggests contradiction.</p>
<ol>
<li><p>The sequence with <m>u_1 = 4</m> and <m>u_{n+1} = 3u_n - 2</m> satisfies <m>u_n = 3^n + 1</m> for every positive integer <m>n</m>.</p><p>If <m>u_k = 3^k + 1</m>, then <m>u_{k+1} = 3(3^k + 1) - 2 = 3^{k+1} + 1</m>, and <m>u_1 = 4 = 3^1 + 1</m>. A proof by induction.</p></li>
<li><p>For every positive integer <m>n</m>, <m>2^n + 1</m> is prime.</p><p>It is false: for <m>n = 3</m>, <m>2^3 + 1 = 9 = 3 \times 3</m>. A disproof by counterexample.</p></li>
<li><p>For every integer <m>n</m> with <m>1 \le n \le 6</m>, <m>n^2 - n + 11</m> is prime.</p><p>There are only six cases: 11, 13, 17, 23, 31 and 41 are all prime. A proof by exhaustion. (For <m>n = 11</m> the value is <m>121 = 11^2</m>, so the statement could not be extended to every <m>n</m>.)</p></li>
<li><p>For every real number <m>x</m>, <m>x^2 - 6x + 10 \gt 0</m>.</p><p>Completing the square gives <m>x^2 - 6x + 10 = (x - 3)^2 + 1</m>, which is at least 1 for every real <m>x</m>. A direct proof.</p></li>
</ol></p></solution>
    <answer><p>proof by induction</p></answer>
    <answer><p>disproof by counterexample</p></answer>
    <answer><p>proof by exhaustion</p></answer>
    <answer><p>direct proof</p></answer>
</stack-static>